---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AppShot from '../components/AppShot.astro';
import { APP_STORE_URL, GUIDE_URL } from '../consts';

const check = '<path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"/>';
---

<Base
  title="How it works — QPushly for Shopify"
  description="How QPushly works end to end: the theme app embed, the browser opt-in, the service worker, the automations, and how a click gets attributed back to an order."
>
  <section class="phero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / How it works</p>
      <h1>From a tap on your storefront to a number on your dashboard</h1>
      <p>
        No black box. Here is every moving part, in the order a shopper meets them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 1. the opt-in ── -->
  <section class="sec pt-4">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Stage 01 · Opt-in</span>
        <h2 class="mt-3.5">A visitor allows notifications</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          The theme app embed loads a small script on your storefront. After a
          delay you choose, your own invite appears. Only if the shopper accepts
          it does the browser show its permission prompt — and when they allow
          that, the browser hands over a push endpoint, an address the browser
          vendor will accept messages for.
        </p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />The endpoint belongs to the browser, not to you — nobody else can send to it</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Your store gets its own signing keypair, so notifications are provably from you</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />No email address or phone number is collected at this point</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Invite wording, delay and position are set in the theme editor</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Or skip the invite and ask from any button with a data attribute</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <div class="rounded-xl2 border p-7" style="border-color:var(--line); background:var(--grad-tint)">
          <div class="anim-optin">
            <div class="op-card">
              <span class="ic"></span>
              <span class="ln"></span>
              <span class="op-btn">Allow</span>
            </div>
            <div class="op-sub">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />
              Subscriber added
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 2. what gets tracked ── -->
  <section class="sec alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr rv">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Stage 02 · Signals</span>
        <h2>Four things the embed reports</h2>
        <p>Only what the automations actually need. Every request is signed by Shopify's app proxy, so nothing else can post data into your store's account.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="steps mt-12 rv">
        <div class="step">
          <span class="sn">Signal</span>
          <h3>Cart changes</h3>
          <p>For subscribers only: a snapshot of the cart — items, value, currency — whenever it changes. This is what makes abandoned-cart recovery possible for a shopper who never gave you an address, and it is closed the moment they order.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <span class="sn">Signal</span>
          <h3>Product views</h3>
          <p>Which product and variant a subscriber looked at, whether it was in stock, and the price at the time. Back-in-stock and price-drop alerts read from this.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <span class="sn">Signal</span>
          <h3>Notification clicks</h3>
          <p>A click carries a campaign tag into the cart as an attribute. When the order is created, Shopify's webhook lets QPushly match order to send.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <span class="sn">Signal</span>
          <h3>“Buy it now” presses</h3>
          <p>“Buy it now” skips the cart, so there is nowhere to carry a tag. The embed notes which notification brought the shopper the moment they press it, so those orders are credited too.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="lead-c mt-10 rv">
        What the embed does <b>not</b> do: call third-party geolocation services,
        load fonts or scripts from another origin, or fingerprint visitors. Cart
        and product tracking can each be switched off in the theme editor if you
        do not use the automations that need them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 3. sending ── -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <div class="rounded-xl2 border p-7" style="border-color:var(--line); background:var(--grad-tint)">
          <div class="anim-clock">
            <span class="cl-face"></span>
            <div class="cl-rows">
              <div class="cl-row done"><span class="cb"></span><b>Scheduled campaigns</b></div>
              <div class="cl-row done"><span class="cb"></span><b>Due reminders</b></div>
              <div class="cl-row done"><span class="cb"></span><b>Stalled carts</b></div>
              <div class="cl-row"><span class="cb"></span><b>Browse abandonment</b></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Stage 03 · Sending</span>
        <h2 class="mt-3.5">A scheduler sweeps every minute</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Nothing depends on you having a tab open. On each pass QPushly picks up
          restocks and price changes Shopify has reported, campaigns whose time has
          come, reminders that are due, carts that have gone quiet and products
          that were viewed and left.
        </p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Sends are signed with your store's own keypair before they leave</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />A subscriber never receives the same reminder twice</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Failures are recorded per subscriber rather than silently dropped</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Endpoints the browser has revoked stop being retried</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── 4. reporting ── -->
  <section class="sec alt">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Stage 04 · Reporting</span>
        <h2 class="mt-3.5">Then it shows up in your admin</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Revenue generated, subscribers, campaigns sent and impressions on the
          dashboard; a growth chart over 7, 15, 30 or 60 days; and per-campaign
          and per-automation numbers wherever you are looking at one.
        </p>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Impressions and clicks per campaign — and per version in an A/B test</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Orders and attributed revenue, matched via order webhooks</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Sends, clicks and revenue per automation</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Each order credited once, even when Shopify reports it again after an edit or refund</li>
          <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" set:html={check} />Subscriber breakdown by browser, OS and device</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rv"><AppShot /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── privacy + platform ── -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr rv">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Housekeeping</span>
        <h2>Where your data lives and when it leaves</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cards mt-12 rv">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Scoped to your store</h3>
          <p>Every record is tagged with your shop domain, and every storefront request is verified against Shopify's signature before it is accepted.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M3 6h18M8 6V4h8v2M6 6l1 14h10l1-14"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Deleted on uninstall</h3>
          <p>Uninstalling removes your access tokens immediately. Shopify then sends a shop-redact request, and every row belonging to your store is deleted.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 16v-4M12 8h.01"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Customer requests handled</h3>
          <p>Shopify's data-request and redaction webhooks are implemented. A request assembles everything held for that shopper into a file you can download from the app; a redaction deletes their record, cart history and attributed orders.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="lead-c mt-10 rv">
        Full detail in the <a href="/privacy" class="underline underline-offset-2">Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec pt-0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta rv">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Get started</span>
        <h2>Two minutes to the <em>first subscriber</em></h2>
        <p>Install, enable the theme embed, open your own storefront and accept the prompt. You will see yourself in the list.</p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
          <a href={APP_STORE_URL} class="btn btn-accent btn-lg">Install free</a>
          <a href={GUIDE_URL} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">Read the guide</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
